01 What is MCP

MCP stands for Model Context Protocol. It is an open protocol released by Anthropic in November 2024.
Think of MCP as a USB-C port for AI models. Before MCP, every AI tool needed a custom integration to connect with external tools like browsers, databases, or file systems. MCP standardizes this — any MCP-compatible AI client can talk to any MCP server.

Two key pieces:

MCP Client — the AI-side application (VS Code with Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code, etc.)
MCP Server — a process that exposes tools (browser control, file access, database queries)
You type a prompt in the client. The client asks the AI which tools it needs. The AI calls the tools through the server. The server executes and returns results.

02 What is Playwright MCP

Playwright MCP is Microsoft's official MCP server that wraps Playwright's browser automation as tools an AI can call.
When connected to Playwright MCP, an AI agent can:

Open URLs
Click buttons, fill forms, hover elements
Read the DOM through accessibility snapshots
Take screenshots
Watch network traffic and console messages
Run tests in a real browser
The key difference from screenshot-based AI browsers: Playwright MCP reads the accessibility tree instead of pixels. This is the same tree a screen reader uses. It is faster, cheaper, and more deterministic — and it is exactly why getByRole is the recommended locator strategy in Playwright.

03 The three agents

Starting from Playwright v1.56 (October 2025), Playwright ships three built-in AI agents.
Each is a Markdown file that instructs the AI how to behave for a specific role.

Role 01

Planner
Explores the app in a real browser and writes a plain-English test plan. Never touches code.

Input
A URL and an instruction like "cover the checkout flow"
Output
A numbered Markdown test plan in specs/\*.md
Constraint
Read-only browser. No destructive clicks. Writes only to specs/.

Role 02

Generator
Turns a plan scenario into runnable Playwright TypeScript that follows your framework conventions.

Input
A plan file and a scenario number
Output
A Playwright .spec.ts file that runs and passes
Constraint
Verifies locators in a live browser before writing them. Asks before creating new page objects.

Role 03

Healer
Diagnoses failing tests and fixes them — without weakening assertions or hiding real bugs.

Input
A failing test file
Output
A minimum-viable fix + a mandatory report explaining what was done
Constraint
Preserves assertion intent. Never softens tests. Never skips silently. Stops after 2 failed attempts.
You can run them individually or chain them: Planner → Generator → Healer.

04 Prerequisites

Before starting, confirm the following:

Node.js 18+ — run node --version
VS Code 1.105 or later — agents need this version
GitHub Copilot signed in with an active model
Playwright 1.56 or later — run npx playwright --version
Do not skip
If any of these are outdated, update them first. Skipping this step causes hours of debugging later — most agent setup failures trace back to a version mismatch here.

05 Installation

Playwright MCP can be installed in two different ways depending on what you want to do. Pick the one that matches your goal.
The two installation methods
Method Command When to use

Method A
Standalone MCP server
code --add-mcp '{"name":"playwright",...}'
Give an AI browser control without using Playwright's built-in agents. Works globally across any project.

Method B
Bundled with agents (recommended)
npx playwright init-agents --loop=vscode
You are writing Playwright tests and want the Planner, Generator, Healer workflow. Runs inside a Playwright project.

Method A gives you only the MCP server. Method B gives you the MCP server plus the three agent definitions, seed test, and workflow files — everything you need for the Planner/Generator/Healer workflow

We do not need both. If we plan to write tests, use Method B.
